 "footnotes": [
  {
   "id": "c-pythagoras",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The right triangle with hypotenuse $a$ and legs $b$ and $c$ gives $b^{2}+c^{2}=a^{2},$ that is $b^{2}=a^{2}-c^{2},$ which turns $\\frac{y^{2}}{a^{2}-c^{2}}$ into $\\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}.$"
  },
  {
   "id": "c-ex20-ref",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book links Equation 11.14, the horizontal hyperbola; the vertical ellipse is <a href=\"#fs-id1167794020407\">Equation 11.13</a>."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-ex21-ref",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book links Equation 11.15, the vertical hyperbola; this horizontal one is <a href=\"#fs-id1167794136510\">Equation 11.14</a>."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-rotated-c",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "In the lines for $C'$ the book prints “$13{\\sin }^{2}60+(-6\\sqrt{3})\\sin 60\\cos 60=7{\\cos }^{2}60$” and then “${(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+6\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})(\\frac{1}{2})+7{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$”; with the formula $C'=A{\\sin }^{2}\\theta -B\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta +C{\\cos }^{2}\\theta$ they are $13{\\sin }^{2}60-(-6\\sqrt{3})\\sin 60\\cos 60+7{\\cos }^{2}60=13{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+6\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})(\\frac{1}{2})+7{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2},$ which is the printed 16."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-hyperbola-caption",
   "kind": "correction",
   "figure": "sim-hyperbola",
   "html": "The caption’s “any point on the ellipse” means any point on the hyperbola."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-e319",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "With $a=\\frac{525.91}{2}=262.955$ and $e=0.995,$ $ep=a(1-e^{2})=262.955(1-{0.995}^{2})\\approx 2.623,$ so $r=\\frac{2.623}{1+0.995\\cos \\theta}.$"
  }
